{
  // maxi-quality — Java editor settings, against layer1-java.
  //
  // READ README §4 BEFORE COPYING THIS. Java is the one language here where
  // the contract cannot deliver parity: the gate is built on Error Prone and
  // NullAway, which are javac plugins, and redhat.java produces its Problems
  // entries with the Eclipse compiler (ECJ). There is no setting that routes
  // Error Prone into the Problems panel. This file therefore pins only what CAN
  // be made honest, and says out loud what it deliberately does NOT pin —
  // which for Java is most of it.

  // DELIBERATELY ABSENT: java.compile.nullAnalysis.mode. An earlier draft
  // pinned it to "disabled" so JDT's own null analysis could not report nulls
  // layer1-java never asserts. That was the wrong trade twice over. Every key
  // in this directory names the CI behaviour it pins; this one would have
  // named the ABSENCE of one, which is a measurement convenience rather than
  // a contract. And it is a working bug-finder — switching it off to make
  // step 3's diff tidier optimises for the measurement over the developer.
  //
  // Its default is "interactive", which ASKS before enabling, so nothing is
  // imposed either way. If you turn it on, its findings are simply outside
  // this contract: README §3 names them as a Problems-panel source no gate
  // produces, which is what step 3 excludes rather than something it has to
  // chase.

  // adopt.sh writes the lint configuration into the consumer's own pom.xml as
  // a marker-delimited region (scripts/pom-region.py), and layer1-java asserts
  // all three fixture poms carry it verbatim. The extension's default here is
  // "interactive" — it waits for a click before re-reading a changed pom — so
  // after an adopt.sh run or an upgrade the editor is working from the old
  // build configuration until someone notices. "automatic" is what makes the
  // pom the editor reads and the pom Maven reads the same file.
  "java.configuration.updateBuildConfiguration": "automatic",

  // DELIBERATELY ABSENT: java.format.settings.url. configs/java/pom-lints.xml
  // formats with Spotless running palantir-java-format in AOSP style. That
  // key takes an Eclipse formatter profile XML, which is a different formatter
  // with different output — pointing it anywhere would make format-on-save
  // fight `mvn spotless:check`. Format with `mvn spotless:apply`; see
  // README §4.
  "[java]": {
    // Off on purpose, for the reason directly above. This is the only language
    // fragment here that turns format-on-save OFF, and it is not an oversight.
    "editor.formatOnSave": false
  }
}
